╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: PureDB IPv6 allow/deny IP rules are silently discarded, bypassing    │
│  per-user deny lists                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: LOW                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 3.1                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/source                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: src/pure-pw.c (record writer) -> src/log_puredb.c (record reader  │
│  and rule evaluation)                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  PureDB records are colon-separated and pure-pw writes per-user allow/deny   │
│  IP patterns verbatim, while the daemon reads the fields positionally. An    │
│  IPv6 address literal contains colons, so configuring one writes extra       │
│  fields: the deny pattern is silently dropped and a client from the denied   │
│  address still authenticates. The allow-list variant shifts a fragment into  │
│  the time-restriction field, which denies every login and also drops the     │
│  allow restriction.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A per-user network restriction configured through the documented            │
│  administrative interface is silently not enforced. Any client holding       │
│  valid credentials for the account can authenticate from the address the     │
│  administrator intended to deny - and, because the rule disappears rather    │
│  than degrading, from any other address as well. For allow lists the         │
│  account becomes unusable, denying the legitimate user while the address     │
│  restriction is also lost. The 1.0.54 changelog states that IP access rules  │
│  now support IPv6 patterns; that does not hold for IPv6 address literals.    │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  A PureDB (virtual-user) record is one line of fields separated by           │
│  PW_LINE_SEP (`:`). The daemon reads it positionally in                      │
│  pw_puredb_parseline(): position 13 is the allowed local IP, 14 denied       │
│  local IP, 15 allowed client IP, 16 denied client IP and 17 the time         │
│  restrictions. pure_strtok2() in src/utils.c performs a plain                │
│  single-character split with no quoting or escaping, and the writer          │
│  add_new_pw_line() in src/pure-pw.c prints each pattern with `%s` without    │
│  validation:\n\n```c title=src/pure-pw.c startLineNumber=711                 │
│  endLineNumber=731\n    if (pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL) {\n              │
│  fprintf(fp2, "%s", pwinfo->allow_local_ip);   /* allowed local ip */\n      │
│  }\n    ...\n    if (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL) {\n                     │
│  fprintf(fp2, "%s", pwinfo->deny_client_ip);   /* denied client ip */\n      │
│  }\n```\n\nWith `-R '::1/128'` the two colons of the literal add two         │
│  separators, so the deny column at position 16 is empty and `1/128` is       │
│  pushed to position 18, which the parser never reads. access_ip_check()      │
│  therefore sees an empty deny list and returns success for every address;    │
│  pure-pw show also reports an empty deny list, so the misconfiguration is    │
│  invisible to the operator. With `-r '::1/128'` (allow list) the fragment    │
│  lands in the time-restriction column, time_restrictions_check() cannot      │
│  parse it and returns -1, so every login is denied while the allow           │
│  restriction is also gone.\n\nThe IPv6 CIDR matcher in access_ip_match()     │
│  (ipv6_cidr_match() plus inet_pton(AF_INET6, ...)) is correct but cannot be  │
│  reached with an IPv6 literal through the product's only rule-writing        │
│  interface, because such a literal can never occupy a single                 │
│  colon-separated field. IPv6 hostname rules contain no colon and do work.    │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the tree and use the in-tree `src/pure-pw` and                     │
│  `src/pure-ftpd`.\n2. Create a virtual user whose client-IP deny list        │
│  contains an IPv6 literal: `pure-pw useradd <login> -f <passwd> -u <uid> -g  │
│  <gid> -d <home> -R '::1/128'`.\n3. Create a control user with an IPv4 deny  │
│  rule: `-R '127.0.0.1/32'`.\n4. Inspect the generated password lines: the    │
│  IPv6 record has extra fields (`...:::::1/128:`) and an empty deny column;   │
│  the IPv4 record is well formed.\n5. Build the database with `pure-pw mkdb   │
│  <puredb> -f <passwd>` and start `pure-ftpd -S <port> -E -j -A -l            │
│  puredb:<puredb>`.\n6. Log in as the IPv6-denied user from the IPv6          │
│  loopback address: the server answers `230 OK`, so the deny rule is not      │
│  enforced.\n7. Log in as the IPv4-denied control user from the IPv4          │
│  loopback address: the server answers `530 Login authentication failed`,     │
│  proving the deny mechanism itself works and only the IPv6 rule was          │
│  dropped.\n8. Log in as the user configured with `-r '::1/128'` (allow       │
│  list): the server answers `530` because the shifted fragment became an      │
│  unparsable time restriction.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3\n"""PureDB IPv6 allow/deny rules are silently        │
│  discarded.\nRun from the repository root as root after building             │
│  src/pure-pw and src/pure-ftpd."""\nimport os, socket, subprocess,           │
│  time\n\nPURE_PW, PURE_FTPD = "./src/pure-pw", "./src/pure-ftpd"\nWORK =     │
│  "/var/tmp/pf-iprule-poc"\nPASSWD, DB = WORK + "/passwd", WORK +             │
│  "/puredb"\nHOME, PORT, UID, PW = "/var/ftproot/user", 2121, 1000,           │
│  "changeme"\n\ndef add_user(login, extra):\n    r =                          │
│  subprocess.run([PURE_PW, "useradd", login, "-f", PASSWD, "-u", str(UID),\n  │
│  "-g", str(UID), "-d", HOME] + extra,\n                       input=(PW +    │
│  "\\n" + PW + "\\n").encode(), capture_output=True)\n    print("useradd      │
│  %-8s %-18s rc=%d" % (login, " ".join(extra), r.returncode))\n\ndef          │
│  login(host, user):\n    s = socket.create_connection((host, PORT),          │
│  timeout=10); s.settimeout(10)\n    def rd():\n        try: return           │
│  s.recv(65535).decode("latin1")\n        except Exception: return ""\n       │
│  rd(); s.sendall(b"USER " + user.encode() + b"\\r\\n"); rd()\n               │
│  s.sendall(b"PASS " + PW.encode() + b"\\r\\n"); r = rd(); s.close()\n        │
│  return r.strip().splitlines()[0] if r else "<none>"\n\nos.makedirs(WORK,    │
│  exist_ok=True)\nadd_user("v6deny", ["-R", "::1/128"])        # documented   │
│  IPv6 deny rule\nadd_user("v4deny", ["-R", "127.0.0.1/32"])   #              │
│  control\nadd_user("v6allow", ["-r", "::1/128"])       # allow-list          │
│  variant\nprint("--- password records ---")\nfor line in open(PASSWD):\n     │
│  print(line.rstrip())\nsubprocess.run([PURE_PW, "mkdb", DB, "-f", PASSWD]);  │
│  os.chmod(DB, 0o644)\nd = subprocess.Popen([PURE_FTPD, "-S", str(PORT),      │
│  "-E", "-j", "-A", "-l", "puredb:" + DB])\ntime.sleep(2)\ntry:\n             │
│  print("::1       v6deny  ->", login("::1", "v6deny"))     # 530 expected,   │
│  230 observed\n    print("::1       v6allow ->", login("::1", "v6allow"))\n  │
│  print("127.0.0.1 v4deny  ->", login("127.0.0.1", "v4deny"))\nfinally:\n     │
│  d.terminate(); d.wait()                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: src/log_puredb.c:436-441                                      │
│    Daemon: reject records that still have fields after the last defined      │
│  column (fail closed)                                                        │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     if (time_restrictions_check(time_restrictions) != 0) {              │
│          return -1;                                                          │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│      return 0;                                                               │
│  }                                                                           │
│    +     if (time_restrictions_check(time_restrictions) != 0) {              │
│          return -1;                                                          │
│      }                                                                       │
│      if (pure_strtok2(&cursor, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) {                      │
│          return -1;              /* malformed record: unexpected trailing    │
│  fields */                                                                   │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│      return 0;                                                               │
│  }                                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: src/pure-pw.c:633-635                                         │
│    pure-pw: refuse to write an IP rule containing the field separator        │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     if (fp2 == NULL) {                                                  │
│          return -1;                                                          │
│      }                                                                       │
│    +     if (fp2 == NULL) {                                                  │
│          return -1;                                                          │
│      }                                                                       │
│      if ((pwinfo->allow_local_ip != NULL &&                                  │
│           strchr(pwinfo->allow_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||           │
│          (pwinfo->deny_local_ip != NULL &&                                   │
│           strchr(pwinfo->deny_local_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||            │
│          (pwinfo->allow_client_ip != NULL &&                                 │
│           strchr(pwinfo->allow_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL) ||          │
│          (pwinfo->deny_client_ip != NULL &&                                  │
│           strchr(pwinfo->deny_client_ip, *PW_LINE_SEP) != NULL)) {           │
│          fputs("IP access rules cannot contain the ':' field separator;\n"   │
│                "use a hostname rule for IPv6 clients instead of an address   │
│  "                                                                           │
│                "literal.\n", stderr);                                        │
│          return -1;                                                          │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Validate the four IP-pattern fields when the record is written so a value   │
│  containing the record separator is rejected with a clear error instead of   │
│  corrupting the record, and make the daemon treat a record with more fields  │
│  than the format defines as invalid so any corrupted or hand-edited entry    │
│  fails authentication rather than dropping its restrictions. Longer term,    │
│  add an escape encoding for the field separator to the PureDB record format  │
│  (writer and reader together, with a version marker) so IPv6 literals can    │
│  be stored and evaluated, and document hostname rules as the supported way   │
│  to express IPv6 restrictions in the meantime. Operators that already        │
│  configured IPv6 literals should re-check affected accounts with pure-pw     │
│  show and migrate to hostname rules.                                         │
│                                                                              │
